   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network works by using a router to connect devices to the internet. The router acts as a central hub, directing all the devices to connect to the internet. When a device connects to the internet, it sends a signal to the router, which then routes the signal to the access point.\n\nThe access point is a device that connects to the router and relays the signal to all the devices in the network. The access point is usually a small device that can be plugged into a wall outlet or a router. The access point uses radio waves to transmit the signal to all the devices in the network.\n\nWhen two devices want to transmit a video stream, they use a protocol called Wi-Fi. This protocol allows the devices to share the same Wi-Fi network and share the same radio frequency. The devices then use the Wi-Fi signal to transmit their video streams to each other.\n\nWhen two devices transmit at once, they use a technique called \"multiplexing.\" This means that the devices can share the same Wi-Fi signal and use it to transmit their video streams simultaneously. This allows the devices to share the same Wi-Fi network and use it to transmit their video streams without interfering with each other.\n\nIn summary, a home Wi-Fi network works by using a router to connect"
